-# Append-only monotonic-key workloads for the by5 resolver harness
-
-## Format: BY5RNG03
-
-A BY5RNG03 trace is a BY5RNG02 trace (same 104-byte fixed header, the long ages, and 32-byte transaction records)
-with two extra header words after the long ages: `pattern` (1 = append-only monotonic keys) and `prefixes`
-(1, 2, 4, 8 or 16). BY5RNG02 traces are read and written exactly as before.
-
-Ranks map to keys with the campaign's `by5::key(rank)`: 12 bytes of `a`, the rank big-endian in bytes 12–15, padded
-with `a` to 16 + 8·(rank mod 7) bytes. Keys are therefore 16–64 bytes long (seven lengths, equally frequent), as in
-the current campaign; they are not all 64 bytes. Lexicographic order of keys equals numeric order of ranks.
-
-With `prefixes = P`, the rank space is split into P equal blocks; prefix p owns ranks
-`[p·2^32/P, (p+1)·2^32/P)` (capped at the header's domain, 0xFFFFFFF0) and writes its keys in increasing order from
-the block start. With P = 1 there is one global sequence.
-
-Records in a BY5RNG03 trace:
-- every write is one new key: `write.end = write.begin + 1` in ranks; it is the next unused rank of its prefix;
-- the harness turns it into the single-key range `[k, k + 0x00)` for every backend (for a 64-byte key the end is
-  65 bytes); FDB and the paged B+ receive the same logical operation, and the B+ recognises it as a point by itself;
-- a read is a range of 1–10 consecutive counter positions (ranks) inside one prefix, within the ranks assigned before
-  the batch started; positions whose writer was rejected stay unused, so a wide read can cover gaps and may contain
-  fewer than its width of inserted keys; a read of
-  width 1 is the single-key range `[k, k + 0x00)` of a key that was inserted (its writer was accepted); a wider read is
-  `[key(begin), key(end))`;
-- a read is empty (`begin = end = 0`, never a conflict) only while no key has been assigned yet (the first batch);
-  the verifier rejects an empty read once keys exist;
-- read version = commit version − 1,562,500 µs (the reference short age); age class 0; the expected conflict flag is
-  exact with respect to the accepted writes of earlier transactions.
-
-## Generator
-
-`generate.cpp`: `by5mono-generate --prefixes 1|16 --seconds S --measure M --seed N --out FILE`.
-- Rate, batch size and read age of the reference: 65,536 transactions per second in batches of 2,048 (32 per second),
-  one read and one write per transaction, read age 1.5625 s, long-age transactions none.
-- The write prefix is drawn uniformly per transaction (P > 1). The read prefix is drawn uniformly among prefixes that
-  already hold keys; the width is uniform in 1–10 (clamped to the keys available); the start is uniform among the
-  positions that keep the read inside the assigned keys. A width-1 read redraws (up to 64 times) until it picks an
-  inserted key; if all 64 draws hit rejected positions, generation fails.
-- Counters: `assignedKeys` are the ranks given to writes (accepted or rejected); `acceptedInserts` are the writes
-  actually applied.
-- No conflict rate is targeted and no read is steered towards or away from recent keys. A key inserted after a read's
-  version and covered by it is a legitimate conflict; its writer is then not applied, and its rank stays unused.
-- Writes are applied to the oracle in transaction order; reads never cover keys of their own batch.
-
-## Verifier
-
-`verify.cpp`: `by5mono-verify FILE` rebuilds everything independently (a per-key array of the batch that inserted
-it, not the generator's interval oracle) and checks: unique keys, strictly consecutive per prefix; point writes;
-reads inside one prefix and inside the keys inserted before the batch; width ≤ 10; width-1 reads of inserted keys;
-read versions; and that each expected conflict flag equals the conflict computed from the accepted writes. It prints
-the read-width distribution, the age of the keys read and the per-prefix counts.
-
-## Tests
-
-`run-tests.sh OUTDIR` (host compiler, `-std=c++17 -Wall -Wextra -Werror -O2`):
-- `keyOrder`: lexicographic order equals rank order for 200,000 random pairs, for 1 and 16 prefixes;
-- `legacyHeader`: a BY5RNG02 header reads and re-writes byte for byte (with `BY5MONO_LEGACY_TRACE`, also an
-  existing trace's header);
-- `monotonicHeader`, `determinism` (same seed: identical bytes; another seed: different), `generatorProperties`;
-- the verifier passes a generated trace and fails four mutants (duplicate key, read of a future key, flipped
-  expected flag, an empty read after keys exist);
-- the legacy-trace check runs only when `BY5MONO_LEGACY_TRACE` is set and non-empty;
-- two 120 s traces per pattern generated twice: identical bytes, verifier PASS;
-- `neighbours.trace`: a hand-built trace (2 transactions per batch) around a 64-byte key `m` and its neighbours
-  `m−1`, `m+1`: reads of `m` conflict after `m` is written, reads of either neighbour and ranges ending exactly at `m`
-  do not, a rejected transaction's write is not applied, and a later transaction in the same batch conflicts with an
-  earlier one's write. The expectations are fixed by hand and checked against the oracle when the trace is built;
-  running it through the harness with `BY5_VALIDATE=1` checks every backend decision against them.
